---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Foundation from "../components/Foundation.astro";
import WaitlistForm from "../components/WaitlistForm.astro";
import logo from "../assets/brand/logo-lockup.png";
import { site } from "../data/site";
---

<Base title={site.name} description={site.description}>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <p class="kicker">Self-paced · Greece, Rome, the Renaissance</p>
    <h1 class="hero-logo">
      <Image
        src={logo}
        alt="Sophandros"
        width={923}
        height={524}
        class="hero-logo-img"
        loading="eager"
      />
    </h1>
    <p class="hero-promise">A modern revival of the classical education</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/curriculum">Curriculum</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">What it is</span>
        <h2>The education of the complete man</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          For centuries the educated elite of Europe and colonial America were
          formed the same way: Latin and Greek, Homer and Plato, Cicero and Virgil,
          and the history of every republic that rose and fell. Harvard’s rules of
          1642 asked a student to read Cicero at sight and decline Greek nouns and
          verbs before he could be admitted.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Founders were products of that education, and they leaned on it when
          they designed a nation. Before the Constitutional Convention, Madison wrote
          notes on the ancient and modern confederacies. Hamilton, Madison, and Jay
          signed the <em>Federalist</em> “Publius,” after a founder of the Roman
          republic.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sophandros revives that education for a working life today. It builds one
          figure layer by layer, the way the ancient world did. First comes a man who
          masters himself, then his household, his city, its law, his own mind, and
          finally the practical wisdom to govern all of them. Each layer widens the
          circle. Rome, the Renaissance, and the American founding carry him out to
          the nation and the world.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Foundation />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">Who it is for</span>
        <h2>People whose weeks are already full</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          It is for a professional who wants the sequence behind the work itself:
          why a practice, a law, or a building looks the way it does. The course
          was shaped around a physician in training, someone who can give the work
          a few hours, set it down, and pick it up again. Several of the Greece
          questions ask what a text has to say to medicine, and to any craft that
          judges particular cases.
        </p>
        <p>
          You want Homer, Plato, Virgil, and Palladio, and enough Latin and Greek to
          meet a sentence in the original. The stated aim is modest. By the end you
          should be able to read adapted Caesar and Cicero, and Virgil with notes.
          Full fluency is not the promise.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">The arc</span>
        <h2>Three phases</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="prose">
        Four parts. Greece is the ideals, from a warrior’s honor to practical
        wisdom, and the prologue and the first chapter can be read now. Rome is
        duty and law. The Renaissance is what a later age made of that inheritance.
        The Birth of an American Nation is the culmination. The later lessons are still to be written.
      </p>
      <div class="phases">
        <article class="phase">
          <span class="phase-num">I</span>
          <h3>Greece</h3>
          <p class="meta-line">About twelve months · Drafted</p>
          <p>
            Six layers, from the warrior’s honor to practical wisdom. Homer through
            Aristotle and the Parthenon.
          </p>
          <a class="more" href="/curriculum#greece">Open the curriculum</a>
        </article>
        <article class="phase">
          <span class="phase-num">II</span>
          <h3>Rome</h3>
          <p class="meta-line">About twelve months · Coming soon</p>
          <p>
            The same person, carried into duty and law: Livy, Cicero, the
            <em>Aeneid</em>, Seneca, Marcus Aurelius, Vitruvius.
          </p>
          <a class="more" href="/curriculum#rome">Open the curriculum</a>
        </article>
        <article class="phase">
          <span class="phase-num">III</span>
          <h3>Renaissance</h3>
          <p class="meta-line">About twelve months · Coming soon</p>
          <p>
            Then the courtier, and the modern professional: Petrarch to Palladio,
            Ascham, Milton, and Montaigne.
          </p>
          <a class="more" href="/curriculum#renaissance">Open the curriculum</a>
        </article>
        <article class="phase">
          <span class="phase-num">IV</span>
          <h3>The Birth of an American Nation</h3>
          <p class="meta-line">Coming soon</p>
          <p>
            The culmination: the nation, looking to the world. Greece, Rome, and
            the Renaissance carried into a new republic.
          </p>
          <a class="more" href="/curriculum#founding">Open the curriculum</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="how-it-works">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">How it works</span>
        <h2>Four steps</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="prose">
        A lesson takes about half an hour. All primary texts are included for
        reference.
      </p>
      <ol class="how-steps">
        <li>
          <span>1</span>
          <h3>Read the lesson here</h3>
          <p>A short essay tells you the context, the ideas, and why they matter.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>2</span>
          <h3>Study the primary text in the reader</h3>
          <p>The public-domain translation is on the same page, with a note on the hard words.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>3</span>
          <h3>Practice the Latin or the Greek</h3>
          <p>One word, and a line to say aloud. A grammar book is optional.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>4</span>
          <h3>Introspection</h3>
          <p>Questions that turn the reading on your own life, and on a problem you face now.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">The languages</span>
        <h2>One word, on the lesson</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each lesson gives you a Greek or Latin word from the reading, and a line
          to say aloud. That is the practice.
        </p>
        <p>
          A grammar book is a way to go further, not a requirement. If you want one
          later: Wheelock’s <em>Latin</em> is grammar first, and Ørberg’s
          <em>Lingua Latina per se Illustrata</em> is reading first. Greek, when you
          come to it, can use <em>Athenaze</em>. None of those books is how you start.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">Introspection</span>
        <h2>Old principles, your problems</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each lesson ends with questions, but they are not a quiz. They ask you to
          look within. When have you faced Achilles’ choice between your pride and
          the people who depend on you? What do you owe when a rule and your
          conscience disagree, as Antigone did? What would you find if you
          examined a belief you have never questioned, the way Socrates did?
        </p>
        <p>
          The ancients worked hard on the same problems you meet at work and at
          home: ambition, anger, duty, loss, how to lead, and how to decide. Looking
          at a problem through their principles often reframes it, and a problem
          seen clearly is far easier to solve.
        </p>
        <p>
          The course is the lessons and the books. Nothing else is required. There
          is also a shorter path, the <a href="/founders">Founders Edition</a>:
          twelve weeks on the Greek and Roman reading behind the American republic.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band" id="waitlist">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">The list</span>
        <h2>Ask to be told when it opens</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="prose">
        Enrollment is not open. Pricing has not been set. Leave an address if you
        want word when the first places are offered.
      </p>
      <WaitlistForm id="home-waitlist" compact />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
